      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Let $p=⟨2,1,6⟩$ and $q=⟨4,2,12⟩.$ Find the vector projection of $p$ onto $q.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{q\\cdot q}q \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}q \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 12}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{12}^{2}}q \\\\  & =\\frac{82}{164}⟨4,2,12⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨4,2,12⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
